# Adding packages

The `howdy add` commands add an optional Howdy package to the project you
are in and wire it up: the dependency in `gleam.toml`, from the same release
as the project's other Howdy packages, the code that configures it, its
place in the app's services, the dev server and the test support, its
migrations after the app's own, and its pages in the dev admin. Each also
adds a guide to `.claude/skills/` and a note to `AGENTS.md`, so a coding
agent knows how to use it.

```sh
howdy add auth
howdy add mail
howdy add flags
howdy add openapi
howdy add remote
```

`howdy add docker` is the exception: it adds no package and downloads
nothing, only the files to [deploy](/docs/guides/deployment) in a container,
for a project made without them.

Each command runs `gleam deps download` afterwards, unless `--skip-deps`
says not to, and takes `--dry-run`, `--json` and `--plain` like the other
commands; see [The howdy command](/docs/cli/overview). A package that keeps
data in the database needs a project made with one.

## Auth

```sh
howdy add auth
```

Adds sign-in and roles with [Howdy Auth](/docs/auth/overview). It writes
`src/<project>/platform/accounts.gleam`, which builds the app's identity and
permissions, and the tests for it, and gives the app:

- sign-in and registration by a link or six-digit code emailed to the
  address, with the account made once the email is used;
- roles, defined in code or in the dev admin, checked in guards;
- the pages `/auth/login`, `/auth/register` and `/auth/account`, and the
  JSON API at `/api/auth`.

The app's services get `identity` and `permissions`, built in `main`, the
dev server and the test support. Auth's migrations run after the app's own.
With the dev admin, `/_howdy` gets users, groups and roles, lets you sign
in as anyone, and previews auth's emails. Guard a controller with
`controller.guarded("/account", auth.required(services.identity))`, and
`ctx.guard.user` is then the user. Set `APP_ORIGIN` to the site's public
address before deploying, as `.env.example` says: links and cookies are for
it.

Sign-in tokens are emailed, so a project without mail gets it too, as with
`howdy add mail`. A project made with `--api-only` gets the JSON API
without the pages, and its emails carry the code without a link.

From then on `--add-auth` on the [`howdy create`](/docs/cli/create)
commands guards what they create.

## Mail

```sh
howdy add mail
```

Adds email with [Howdy Mail](/docs/mail/overview), and smail for writing
templates. It writes:

- `src/<project>/platform/email.gleam`, the mailer `gleam run` uses, built
  from the environment;
- `src/<project>/emails/welcome_email.gleam`, an example email, and its
  preview;
- `src/<project>/emails/previews.gleam`, every email built from sample
  data, and a test that each can be sent.

A mailer goes in the app's services, so controllers send with
`services.mailer`. Under `gleam run` it sends through `SMTP_URL`,
`RESEND_API_KEY` or `SENDGRID_API_KEY`, whichever is set, and logs each
email when none is. Under `gleam dev` email is kept in an outbox in
`tmp/mail`, which the dev admin shows under Mail with the previews. Under
`gleam test` it is kept in an in-memory outbox. Add more emails with
[`howdy create email`](/docs/cli/create).

## Flags

```sh
howdy add flags
```

Adds [feature flags](/docs/flags/overview) with Howdy Flags, kept in the
app's database. It writes:

- `src/<project>/feature_flags.gleam`, the flags with an example,
  `early_access`, and their starting;
- `src/tasks/flags.gleam`, which manages them from the command line:
  `gleam run -m tasks/flags help`;
- a test that turns the example on for one user.

The app's services get `features`, started in `main`, the dev server and
the test support, and the flags' migrations run after the app's own. With
the dev admin, `/_howdy` gets Flags: kill switches, rules, groups and
history. Check a flag with
`flags.enabled(services.features, feature_flags.early_access(), for: flags.user(id))`.

## OpenAPI

```sh
howdy add openapi
```

Adds an [OpenAPI document](/docs/openapi/overview) with Howdy OpenAPI. It
writes `src/<project>/platform/api_docs.gleam`, with the document's title,
version and security schemes, and a test that the document and the
reference page are served. The app serves, after its controllers:

- `/openapi.json`, the document;
- `/docs`, a reference page to read it.

The document lists the routes written with `howdy/openapi/endpoint`, from
the schemas they read. Existing controllers are left as they are, so it
starts empty: move a route to an endpoint to publish it. With the dev
admin, `/_howdy` finds the document and lists every endpoint under API,
where you can call them.

## Remote

```sh
howdy add remote
```

Adds typed calls between services with
[Howdy Remote](/docs/remote/procedures). It writes:

- `src/<project>/procedures.gleam`, the procedures the app serves: a name,
  an input and an output;
- `src/<project>/platform/rpc.gleam`, the server that answers them, and the
  RPC token;
- a test that calls the example procedure both ways.

The procedures are served two ways. Over the cluster, `main` and the dev
server start the server, so other nodes connected to this one call it with
`remote.cluster()`. Over HTTP, the app mounts `POST /rpc/<procedure name>`
for callers that send `authorization: Bearer $RPC_TOKEN`; without
`RPC_TOKEN` it refuses them all. The token is in the app's services as
`rpc_token`. Call another service with
`remote.call(target, procedure, input, timeout: 5000)`.

## Docker

```sh
howdy add docker
```

Adds the files `howdy new` writes for containers, to a project made with
`--no-docker` or by an older CLI:

- `Dockerfile`, which builds an image of the app on the Gleam and
  Erlang/OTP versions installed here, with a `HEALTHCHECK` on `/up`;
- `.dockerignore`, which keeps `build/`, `.env` and local databases out of
  it;
- `bin/start`, which starts the app in the image: `run`, `migrate` with a
  database, and `remote`.

A file the project already has is left as it is, and the command says
which. If `app()` does not mount a
[health check](/docs/guides/health-checks), one is added at `/up`, which the
image's `HEALTHCHECK` asks. Two things in an older project it leaves for
you, and says how:

- a `main` that starts the server with `howdy.start` rather than
  `howdy.start_application`, so `SIGTERM` cuts off requests in flight; see
  [Supervision and shutdown](/docs/guides/supervision);
- with a database, a missing `src/tasks/migrate.gleam`, which
  `bin/start migrate` runs.

## Flags

- `--skip-deps`: do not run `gleam deps download` after changing
  `gleam.toml`.
- `--dry-run`: show which files would be created and modified, without
  changing the project.
- `--json`: print the result as one JSON object: the files created and
  modified, steps left to do by hand, and what to do next.
- `--plain`: print progress as plain lines instead of animating.
